WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:10.000
It is very beautifully expressed by a great hacham, that wrote a book, sometimes somebody should translate it into English, Shlomo Ibn Verga.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Shlomo Ibn Verga was a Jew who left Spain in 1492, like all the other Jews, and wrote an extraordinary book called Shevet Yehuda.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Shevet means a staff, the staff of Judah, but the staff of Judah is how God hit us, the tribe of Judah, because we are the tribe of Judah, over the Galut.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:53.000
And the book is divided into 68 stories, written like a modern short story teller, based upon incidents, upon historical incidents.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:03.000
The incident is historical, but when he says that Shimon said this to Levi, this is made up, because nobody was there.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:27.000
But historical events, and he wants to show through these 68 events, that we, not God, not mysticism, not Galut, not Gilgul, we were directly responsible for the disasters of our history.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
And he has a fantastic word that he uses, it is Hasibba Hativ'it.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000
The thesis is that history has causality, just like you have in physics, yes?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Of course, in effect, history, whether it is collective of the people, or individual, yes?

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:02.000
The causes directly affect, they would yield to results, and those results are as sure as the law of gravity.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.000
It's very interesting, I don't know why, because on the one hand, we believe in miracles.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Nature means that sometimes God could interfere with the laws of nature.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:34.000
God could split the sea, God could bring, I don't know, a locust at a certain time, God could expel the locust, God could make the river into blood.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.000
But not in the realm of history. Why?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
The source for this point is in Arambam.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Arambam says like this, it's a question.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The Torah says, when we came out from Egypt,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:05.000
He says, he didn't want to go directly to Israel, because they would face the enemy, the people would be afraid, and then would return to Egypt.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.000
So Arambam has a question, God's made so many miracles, why he did not make the Jew courageous?

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Why didn't he make them strong?

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:26.000
And Arambam says, because God makes miracles in the realm of physics, but not in human relationship.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.000
In the realm of human, we have free choice.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.000
No miracles would happen there.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.000
The things inside you, or between you and your friends, or between groups in society, they are up to us.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
We could choose, we could choose anything we want to.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.000
And therefore, there are no miracles there.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
You harvest what you sow.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:03.000
And if you plant this, if you plant that and you did not irrigate it and so on, in your own life, the result would be exactly what you did.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:20.000
And therefore, what Rabbi Sholem Oinberg shows, through 68 episodes, that whenever we have a choice, or whenever we have a choice, we invariably chose the less beneficial one.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.000
And that is the cause of the Galut.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And therefore, the opposite of Galut is what I call political repentance.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Mashiach will come, not through a miracle, but when we as a society, we as individuals, change and decide to make our life according to the precepts of the Torah.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:57.000
One of the fantastic things about the history of Israel is that we are the only people that has its history recorded from the beginning of time, every single step.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:08.000
And includes not only the glorious moments in our kingdoms, etc., but also the failure.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.000
We are the only people who recorded the birth of the nation and the death of the nation.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.000
It is simply extraordinary.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:27.000
We don't know the very beginning of England, how England came to be, how Greece came to be, or how, I don't know, every nation.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.000
We know a little bit about America, that's the truth.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
America is a different nation.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
But the nations of the world will know the birth of Egypt.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.000
When we talk about the birth of these nations, we always have myths.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:50.000
Judaism we have from Mr. Abraham, and who was the father of Abraham, and who was the this, and so on.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:02.000
And how from Abraham we built the Hamikdash, the Roman Malekh, and all the flaws and the mistakes that we made, and finally, how the great Hamikdash was destroyed.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Is it clear, this point?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.000
Is it clear, this point?

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
We are history, we don't have no myth.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.000
And therefore, the rebirth of Israel, it has to be through a process.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Not a mythological process, but a process in which we have to chosavit shema.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:47.000
And beautifully, Ahram Bam says that the purpose of Shab-e-Ab, etc., is to remind us of our flaws, so that we could have what I call collective, or to be proper English, political teshuva.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.000
Let me read what Ahram Bam says.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.000
There are days, yes, when all of Israel complains about them.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.000
From the faces of the ministers, they complain about them.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.000
And the purpose of this complaint is to awaken the hearts, to awaken us.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.000
We are in tupitut, we are sleeping, yes, we need to be awakened.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:33.000
To open the paths of repentance, so that we remember, during these fast days, our stupid, evil, nonsensical behavior.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:47.000
The whole idea that our ancestors were flawless, and somehow, something happened.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.000
It's wrong.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:08:03.000
It was so nonsensical, their behavior, as we today.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:11.000
And that behavior, garam.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:19.000
And garam is a Hebrew word, which means natural causality.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.000
It's best said in a single word, that's the greatness of Ahram Bam.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Yes, garam.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.000
Lahem ve'lano etasarot.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:38.000
So, the point of echa, yes, ta'anit echa, is exactly the opposite of myth.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.000
And the cure of it is, remember the stupidities that we made.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.000
Yes, and remember how to open the paths for teshuva.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.000
Now, I have a question.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:58.000
It says, Ahram Bam, the evil behavior of our parents, and us today.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:06.000
Obviously, they ate kashar, they prayed three times a day, they worshiped Meshabbat, etc.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.000
So, what were the haverot that they made?

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Political haverot.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.000
Lack of organization.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.000
There is a way how to organize ourselves.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.000
The Torah tells us exactly how, what are the laws for collective organization.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.000
How to have a sane community.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
How to have a good leadership.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.000
How to render justice.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:35.000
How to protect the weak and the feeble, etc.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.000
So, the Torah says this very clearly.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.000
How to behave with our neighbors, Jewish gentiles.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.000
The Torah tells us this.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:52.000
Now, the haverot that we were making them, was not that somebody ate cheese and meat.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.000
It never was such a thing.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:02.000
Shlomo Ibeberka says, there is not one person who doesn't make abdalá in his time.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.000
Everyone makes abdalá.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.000
So, what are the haverot?

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.000
The haverot are the, what I call, political haverot.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Yes?

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.000
And I'm not a political Zionist.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.000
I'm not a political Zionist.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.000
I believe in Israel because we say, I'm a hazish in adoration three times a day.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.000
I mean, I don't deal with this.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.000
But, something happened in 1948.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:36.000
In which, you know, we don't know what's, you know, as I said to, oh, somebody came to visit me today.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
We were in Spain for 800 years.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.000
The cream of the crop.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.000
800 years.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.000
Yes.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.000
Longer than in Brooklyn.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.000
Yes.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.000
And yet, things happened and we had to change addresses.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.000
Zip code and so on.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:12.000
And, part of the process is that, again, is that we are not aware of what I call political laws.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.000
How to behave in haverot.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.000
Etc.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:20.000
That is the point.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.000
I'm going to have a few questions and then we're going to study the book of Job.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.000
No?

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.000
I don't know where you want to study.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:32.000
But, I thought this should rephrase our studies.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.000
Any questions?

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.000
God knows in advance how human beings are going to act.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:40.000
Yes.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.000
That's the old question.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:47.000
God knows the future and yet we have freedom and how to reconcile this.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.000
I don't know.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
But, we could ask this question in every single point.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.000
I don't know.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:56.000
Yes.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.000
You said no jokes.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.000
That's why I said also, not on my part, also on the audience.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
But, God knows, I don't know.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.000
Yes.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:14.000
Should we pray to have a better memory so we can remember things that we should keep in mind moving forward?

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Or, is that a double-edged sword where you remember things you shouldn't want to remember because it's too painful?

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:30.000
Part of the teshuvah, what I call teshuvah, it means, number one, is acknowledging your responsibility.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:38.000
I have a fantastic derasha, defining the first avon, the avon of Allama Rishon.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:40.000
What's the avon?

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:42.000
He ate the fruit, the forbidden fruit.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.000
Pay attention.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:46.000
The derasha doesn't tell you what the fruit is.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:51.000
If you want to be germane to the story, the derasha will tell you what the fruit was.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.000
What the fruit was, it's irrelevant.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.000
The avon is not eating the fruit.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.000
The avon is when God asks him, why did you eat the fruit?

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.000
He places the blame on the wife.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.000
He asks the wife, why did you eat the fruit?

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.000
He places the blame on the derashash.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.000
The avon is the inability to accept responsibility.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.000
God tells you not to eat the fruit.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.000
There are no excuses.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.000
You have to assume responsibility.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:31.000
When you refuse to assume responsibility on what you do, that is the primary avon.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:38.000
And teshuvah is to say hatati, aviti, me.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:45.000
Not my mother, my father, the school, the country, the nation, this, that.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:47.000
I am the avon.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:59.000
If you assume responsibility, then you may be able to find ways in which to remedy this.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.000
And then you can say to God, hashivenu, adonai lecha v'nashuvah.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:10.000
We ask God to help us in the way that we are forging to accomplish our goal.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:12.000
That is important. We do.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:14.000
And God will help us.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:19.000
But you cannot be, when you do not accept responsibility for your actions,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:25.000
that God is going to, because then he will be creating a big kolel,

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:32.000
in which nobody is responsible for what happens and nobody knows what is going on.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.000
So God didn't want to create a kolel.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.000
No. Accept responsibility.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.000
Yes, and that is the most fantastic thing.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.000
He says, you come out from Gan Eden and then says, beze'at apecha tocha lehem.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.000
Beze'at apecha tocha lehem.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.000
Fantastic.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.000
Means, you have to do it yourself.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:00.000
The flavor, the nutrition of the lehem is a function of beze'at apecha.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:04.000
You work, you work hard, you will accomplish.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.000
And that is the remedy.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.000
That is the fantastic thing.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:10.000
Just read the Torah.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.000
Beze'at apecha tocha lehem.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.000
And that is the thing of teshuva.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Why there is personal teshuva?

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.000
We believe there is collective teshuva.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.000
When we say, you know what?

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:27.000
The way we have been acting in the last X years is nonsense.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.000
Yes? It is stupid.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.000
We don't have problems with the way we are acting.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:35.000
Yes?

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:40.000
And instead of looking for some mythological escape,

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.000
I am going to put in Kippah, Kippah from Imam Yosef,

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.000
but that is not going to solve the political, social,

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:50.000
ben adam l'chaviro, problems.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.000
Yes?

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.000
And that doesn't...

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.000
So, teshuva is, this is the purpose of the ta'anid.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.000
Ta'anid is, look, what?

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.000
The Beth Amigas was destroyed.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:06.000
By whom?

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.000
Our own stupidity.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:10.000
Our own stupidity.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:12.000
And when you go look in the past,

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.000
and Gerush Sefarat as well, one,

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.000
see, rivalry between Jews and so on.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:20.000
The intelligent Jews, interesting,

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.000
I point out in my new book in the Holocaust Society,

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.000
with all this shegaon madness,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.000
the result of this madness of the anti-Mammonian group

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:34.000
was that all those people who were very savvy

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:40.000
in handling monarchs and ministers and so on,

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.000
who had an education,

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
knew the language of the country,

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.000
they were put aside as a picolosim.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:52.000
And then they came to people singing all of this,

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:54.000
and it was a catastrophe.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
And people didn't understand that you have,

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.000
the reason why for 800 years we were all right in Sefarat

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:06.000
is because our leaders knew exactly

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:10.000
how to handle the world at large.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:16.000
And this, that there was peace,

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:21.000
was a result of serious leaders

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.000
committed to the welfare of the community.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:26.000
It doesn't happen like that.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.000
Nothing happens like that.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.000
And so in that sense,

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:36.000
instead of this you have people singing and dancing and so on,

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.000
the mystical thing,

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.000
and they're going to bring the Mashiach,

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.000
fine, very good,

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:44.000
but when they have to guide,

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.000
imagine if you have one of these guys running a grocery.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.000
I mean, it doesn't work.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:54.000
It doesn't work.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.000
So the problem is that we brought the problem unto ourselves.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.000
And so I think I saw,

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.000
you wanted, yes.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:09.000
I'm wondering now, I'm thinking a lot about it now.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:14.000
We're seeing some of the symptoms of our political,

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.000
you were talking about communal political violence.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:22.000
What do you think are the core problems

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.000
and how do we rectify that?

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.000
But I just wanted to give you an example about,

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.000
in Halab there was no anti-Semitism.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.000
I mean, they were only late when they were waiting for Israel.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:36.000
I'll give you an example.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:39.000
You know that Halab is sitting in Sham.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.000
The houses, Jewish houses,

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.000
from the outside, they all look alike.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:49.000
No house from the outside

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.000
could appear as ostentatious.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.000
Halab.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.000
Inside your house, put any furniture you want to.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:18:59.000
All right?

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.000
Outside has to look like the plainest of all houses.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.000
You could enter into the house of Mr. Millionaire Silveira Pichotto,

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:11.000
but from the outside it looks the same as Mr. Hayat,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.000
I don't know, whatever it is, Mr. Shahashkov, whatever it is.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:16.000
So it's the same.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:18.000
Why was that?

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.000
Because if a woman, people didn't know,

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.000
Hakha Matlub spoke about it many times,

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:26.000
to me as well,

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.000
and he mentions it in the introduction

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.000
to his grandfather's Seirot Mishuvot,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.000
that women were permitted to enter the synagogue

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:39.000
with a dress up to a certain amount of money.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.000
Those days it was four liras or whatever it is.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:44.000
I don't know.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.000
Not more than that.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.000
Why not?

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:52.000
Because if a woman comes dressed in an ostentatious way,

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.000
the other wife,

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:56.000
look at this wife, look at this,

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.000
